# Bug: a type declared in the file loses its constructor to a same-named
# function of an imported module.
#
# Run: kanso play type_constructor_hidden_by_import.kso
#
# should: print `true`. The file declares `type tally` with three fields, and
#   a local function named `tally` with three parameters is found the same
#   way with std/list imported (the 2026-10-12 gavel: a local arm shadows).
# does: error[arity]: no 3-argument arm of `tally` (arms take 1). The one
#   arm it sees is `list/tally`. Dropping the import makes it run.
import "std/list"

type tally
  a
  b
  c

t = tally false false true

print "{t.c} {list/sum [1 2]}"
